    "description": "Namak Lake is a largely dry saline lake at the terminus of an internally drained basin in north-central Iran. Its setting connects mountain-fed rivers and heavily used upstream catchments with an evaporative lowland landscape south of Tehran. [s1]",
    "tags": ["Salt lakes", "Closed basins", "Iranian Plateau", "Hydrology", "Namak basin"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:04:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:04:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Namak Lake: Salt, Seasonal Water and a Closed Basin | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Understand Namak Lake’s saline landscape, its mountain-to-basin water connections, and why published lake-area figures require careful interpretation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "lake-and-basin",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A lake defined by its basin",
        "content": "Namak Lake, recorded as Daryacheh-ye Namak in the International Association for Great Lakes Research inventory, is a salt lake in Iran’s arid interior. Its name should be tied to this specific location: a generic reference to a salt lake is not enough to identify the feature. The association’s profile places it in a cold-desert climate and classifies its water as saline. [s2]\n\nThe Freshwater Ecoregions of the World account describes the surrounding drainage basin as endorheic, meaning that its waters terminate inland. It distinguishes Namak from Howz-e Soltan, another salt lake in the same depression. These are related geographic features, not interchangeable names for a single water surface. Understanding that distinction helps prevent photographs, measurements and environmental claims from being assigned to the wrong lake. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "mountain-water",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From mountain catchments to an evaporative lowland",
        "content": "The basin is framed by the Alborz Mountains to the north and the Zagros to the west. Its river network includes the Karaj and Jajrud catchments and the Qom and Qareh Su rivers. Snowmelt from surrounding mountains helps supply the drainage system, while the lowland terminus is largely dry. The ecoregion account describes spring inundation followed by evaporation into summer. This is a seasonal description, not a guarantee of water in a particular year. [s1]\n\nThe contrast between uplands and lowlands is also important in hydrological research. A peer-reviewed study of the Namak basin describes predominantly arid lowlands alongside wetter highland environments. Basin-wide averages can therefore conceal differences between where precipitation falls, where runoff forms and where water ultimately collects. The lake is the downstream expression of a much larger landscape. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measuring-change",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why a single area figure can mislead",
        "content": "Published inventories do not provide one consistent surface-area figure for Namak. The IAGLR profile and the ecoregion account report different values without a common observation date or a shared measurement protocol. They should not be interpreted as successive measurements demonstrating expansion or contraction. A defensible comparison would first need to establish whether each figure represents open water, the lakebed or another mapped boundary. [s1][s2]\n\nFor this reason, this page does not present a fixed current area or turn those inventory entries into a trend. The same caution applies to depth: a catalogue entry does not describe the spatial distribution of water across a shallow seasonal basin, and it cannot establish conditions at the research cutoff. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "hydrological-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading evidence of water stress",
        "content": "Research on low streamflows provides a more specific view of changing water conditions upstream. A study covering 1970–2015 examined seasonal low flows at hydrometric stations across the Namak basin and found widespread declines. It investigated associations with rainfall and temperature, including differences between seasons. Those findings concern river-flow behavior over the study period; they are not measurements of the lake’s annual area. [s3]\n\nThe distinction matters when interpreting environmental change. Evidence that low flows declined can support concern about the basin’s hydrology, while assigning a precise share of lake drying to climate, withdrawals or individual dams requires additional analysis. Namak is most clearly understood through these connected scales: the saline terminal lake, the river network feeding its basin and the climatic conditions influencing that network. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      }
    ],
