    "description": "Iran’s water geography combines mountain-fed rivers, internally drained basins and groundwater systems that sustain settlements and agriculture. Understanding water availability requires distinguishing seasonal flows, stored water, extraction and replenishment rather than treating them as interchangeable measures. [s1][s2][s4]",
    "tags": ["Rivers", "Groundwater", "Water resources", "Drainage basins", "Qanats", "Water management"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:04:07Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:04:07Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Rivers, Groundwater and Water Resources | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s contrasting drainage regions, groundwater systems, qanats and water infrastructure, with guidance on interpreting water-resource evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "drainage-geography",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Where water goes",
        "content": "Iran’s rivers make more sense when read through drainage basins: areas whose runoff converges toward a common destination. Much of the country drains internally, while western mountain catchments carry water toward the Persian Gulf. In the north, streams descend toward the Caspian Sea. Although these rivers leave their local mountain basins, the Caspian itself has no outlet to the world ocean. [s1]\n\nThis geography creates pronounced regional contrasts. The northern slopes of the Alborz receive more moisture than the plateau to their south. In the west, Zagros catchments gather water into extensive river systems. Interior and eastern regions generally have much more limited surface runoff. A map showing river channels alone therefore gives an incomplete impression of dependable water availability. [s1]\n\nSeasonality matters as much as direction. Rain, snow storage and subsequent melt influence when rivers carry water. A river’s spring flow and its late-summer minimum describe different conditions; neither should be substituted for its annual discharge. These distinctions are fundamental to comparing rivers, reservoirs and the demands placed on them. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "groundwater-connections",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The water beneath the river map",
        "content": "Groundwater is water stored below the surface in geological formations called aquifers. Research on Iran distinguishes fractured and dissolved limestone systems in the west, alluvial aquifers in sediment-filled plains, and coastal aquifers beside marine and inland waters. Western karst aquifers help recharge rivers including the Dez, Karun and Karkheh. Surface water and groundwater are thus connected parts of a hydrological system. [s2]\n\nA national study using water-management records from 2002–2015 documented groundwater depletion and salinization. Its historical coverage is important: it provides evidence of sustained pressure during that interval, not a direct reading of aquifer conditions in 2026. The authors also explained that later data with comparable spatial and temporal resolution were unavailable to their analysis. [s2]\n\nThe study found that declining extraction could accompany worsening resource conditions. Less water pumped does not necessarily mean successful conservation if depletion or salinity has reduced usable supplies. Interpreting withdrawal statistics therefore requires evidence about water levels, quality and recharge alongside the amount extracted. The paper discusses both physical constraints and possible management effects rather than treating a falling withdrawal total as self-explanatory. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "qanat-system",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Qanats: infrastructure and shared allocation",
        "content": "A qanat conveys groundwater through a gently sloping underground tunnel, allowing gravity to carry it from an aquifer toward a lower outlet. UNESCO’s account of the Persian Qanat describes systems tapping alluvial aquifers near valley heads and supplying agricultural land and permanent settlements in arid regions. Their significance lies in making groundwater accessible through a built landscape of tunnels and associated facilities. [s3]\n\nUNESCO also emphasizes communal systems for sharing and distributing water. The heritage is consequently institutional as well as technical: maintaining a channel and allocating its discharge are related tasks. This description concerns the selected World Heritage systems and their documented traditions. It should not be read as proof that every surviving qanat has adequate water, remains operational or follows identical allocation arrangements. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "measuring-resources",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading water statistics correctly",
        "content": "FAO’s AQUASTAT separates water resources, withdrawals, irrigation, wastewater and related indicators. These answer different questions. A resource estimate describes availability under a defined method; a withdrawal figure describes abstraction; an irrigation-area measure describes land equipped or used under its stated definition. They cannot be exchanged simply because all concern water. [s4]\n\nThe inspected AQUASTAT database guide also explains a reporting structure that groups observations into multi-year periods and displays the latest available observation within each period. Such entries must retain their actual reference years. They should not be expanded into a complete annual record by repeating values across intervening years. [s4]\n\nFor this guide, annual charts require comparable observations with clear dates and definitions. The sources examined establish the importance of groundwater monitoring and basin geography, but they did not yield a validated annual national series for the latest fifteen-year window. Presenting that gap explicitly is more informative than a chart whose apparent continuity exceeds the evidence. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "explore-branches",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Explore the systems in detail",
        "content": "The pages in this branch follow the Karun, Zayandeh Rud, Sefidrud, Aras, Karkheh and Helmand individually, alongside separate guides to dams and reservoirs and to springs and waterfalls. Each offers a different entry point into the same questions: where water originates, where it travels, how its timing changes and which uses depend on it.\n\nBegin with the basin, then examine the evidence at the appropriate scale. A national groundwater study, a river gauge and a reservoir observation each reveal something useful, but none alone describes the whole system.",
        "source_ids": []
      }
